ion-introduction xpfskxn"><h2 class="xhz5zt5">What it is</h2><p class="xuvmvsv">The drop is the last hundred feet and the part that gets touched most. It runs an aerial span, down a wall, through an entry point and into a room, so it needs to bend tighter than outside plant cable ever does and survive being handled by a crew working fast. Bend spec and termination method decide the labor cost per drop, which is the number that actually matters at volume.</p></div><div class="specification-table xcqzusx fb"><div class="construction-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Product codes</p><p class="xcclquv">Flat drop, flat drop with tracing wire, armored drop NSS and SS, FTTR low-visibility, GYFXBTC8F</p></div><div class="strength-member-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Fiber type</p><p class="xcclquv">G.657A1 and G.657.A2 bend-insensitive, G.657.B3 available</p></div><div class="fibre-type-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Fiber counts</p><p class="xcclquv">1F to 12F</p></div><div class="fibre-counts-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Construction</p><p class="xcclquv">Flat drop, spiral armored, round in and outdoor</p></div><div class="sheath-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Jacket and protection</p><p class="xcclquv">Rodent resistant armor, UV stable, indoor and outdoor rated</p></div><div class="deployment-row xcj10xy fb"><p class="xbslay4">Deployment</p><p class="xcclquv">Aerial span, duct, wall entry, in-home and FTTR</p></div><div class="standards-row xr2tetp"><p class="xbslay4">Lead time</p><p class="xcclquv">6 to 8 weeks from PO, up to 10 weeks for custom builds</p></div></div></section><section class="fibre-count-range xqjs4fo"><h2 class="xqmzbdo">Counts and variants</h2><p class="xbt6gdt">Standard drop counts, with and without tracing wire, pre-connectorized or supplied for field termination.</p><div class="count-rail xhrktuh"><div class="1f xvahfb7"><p class="xzxqqpp">1F</p></div><div class="2f xvahfb7"><p class="xzxqqpp">2F</p></div><div class="4f xvahfb7